{"uncertainty": 0.15, "outcome": "rtl_bug", "artifacts": [{"kind": "root_cause_analysis", "title": "Root cause: debounce counter restarts only on a rising edge of noisy", "summary": "Requirement [requirement:req-19189b08df]. Failure under study: the triage repro fails 1 of 1 checks and reaches $finish at 175000 [evidence:prj-a26914b30e.triage.e2] [run:run-0002]. VeriTriage classified the log as unknown_failure at only 30% confidence, with a top hypothesis of RTL bug [evidence:prj-a26914b30e.triage.e1] [run:run-0001]. I treated that hypothesis as one candidate and did not take it as a conclusion.\n\nDefect, from reading the approved RTL [artifact:prj-a26914b30e.design-under-investigation.a1]: the header says 'A change on noisy, in either direction, restarts the count, so a pulse of STABLE edges or fewer, high or low, never reaches clean'. The code restarts cnt only on 'noisy && !prev', which is a rising change. On a falling change, cnt keeps its value. Once noisy has been high for a long time, cnt is saturated at STABLE-1.\n\nCausality: when noisy falls, nothing resets cnt. On the next edge, noisy==prev and cnt==STABLE-1 are both true, so clean drops to 0 after one stable low edge. A low pulse of 2 edges therefore reaches clean, and the specified filtering fails. This is the fault (a stale cnt) that becomes visible on clean.\n\nIncorrectness: the restart condition should be any change, noisy != prev, as the header clause states. The asymmetry also explains why rising glitches are filtered while falling glitches are not.\n\nRanked hypotheses and the evidence that would refute each:\n(1) rtl_bug. Supported by the code reading above. It would be refuted if a spec-derived low-glitch check passed on this RTL.\n(2) testbench_bug. Rejected for the new repro, because its expected values come from the header clause and not from the RTL's output. It would be supported if the repro also failed on clean RTL; the evaluation's clean-input run tests this.\n(3) infrastructure. Rejected: the triage run built, reached $finish and gave a deterministic check count [run:run-0002]. It would be supported if a clean rebuild with no other change gave a different verdict.\n(4) spec_ambiguity. Rejected: the clause explicitly says 'in either direction' and 'high or low'. It would be supported only if the governing spec lacked that clause.\n\nRepro: tb_debounce_fall_restart.v."}], "files": [{"path": "tb_debounce_fall_restart.v", "kind": "testbench", "title": "Self-checking repro: a short low pulse must not reach clean", "summary": "Minimal self-checking repro for the defect in [artifact:prj-a26914b30e.design-under-investigation.a1]. After reset it holds noisy high for 20 cycles and checks clean==1. It then drives a 2-edge low pulse (fewer than STABLE=4) and checks that clean stays 1 for 14 cycles, as the header clause requires. Last, it holds noisy low and checks clean==0, so a stuck output cannot pass. Expected values come from the spec text, not from RTL output. The expected result is that the glitch check fails on this RTL and all checks pass on an RTL that restarts on any change. It prints the single NIRMAAN-SIM verdict with the run's nonce just before $finish. It serves [requirement:req-19189b08df].", "entry": "tb_debounce_fall_restart"}], "tool_runs": ["run-0001", "run-0002"], "claims": ["tb_debounce_fall_restart.v is expected to fail one check (the low-glitch check) on debounce_rise_only_restart.v and pass all three checks on an RTL that restarts the count on any change of noisy; this has not been run by me.", "The fix is to change the restart condition from (noisy && !prev) to (noisy != prev)."], "escalation": null, "notes": "Outcome is rtl_bug. I did not run any simulation. The platform will run tb_debounce_fall_restart.v against the approved RTL with expect=fail, and that run is the evidence for whether the repro gate is met. Independent review of this analysis has not been recorded yet. The existing review record covers only the design fixture, 